.TH CMEMPOOL_TOTAL_CAPACITY 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cmempool_total_capacity \- report the number of elements a pool holds
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

size_t cmempool_total_capacity(cmempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the total number of pool-owned elements that mp holds, the ones
in use and the free ones together. A dynamic fallback entry is not
counted; cmempool_dynamic_allocs_count(3) reports those.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp                        Memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the number of elements that the pool holds
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This is the elem_count that you gave to cmempool_create_mp(), or for a
pool from cmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp() the element
count that the library worked out from the buffer. It stays the same
for the whole lifetime of the pool.

A thread-safe pool that is large enough to keep a cache for each
thread allocates extra elements above this count as a reserve, so that
the advertised count stays available to any thread however many
entries other threads keep in their caches. This call doesn't report
the reserve, and the reserve doesn't change the pool's memory use at
run time: cmempool_allocated_bytes(3) reports the whole block,
reserve included, and that block is allocated once, when the pool is
created.

For such a pool the two ends of the count are not equally exact. A
thread takes an entry from its own cache with no lock held, so there is
no count to consult at that moment, and entries go into a cache before
anybody knows whether they will be needed. That choice keeps the
guarantee above: the pool never refuses a caller while another thread's
cache holds entries that the first caller was entitled to. The price is
that concurrent callers can hold a few entries more than this count for
a short time, but never more than the physical slot count. A pool with
no cache is exact in both directions. Every pool created with
single_threaded = true is one, and so is every pool built by
cmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp(3); in those an allocation
past this count fails, or falls back to the system allocator.

cmempool_create_mp(3) explains how the pool size affects performance.

A NULL mp is a fatal error: the call prints a message and ends the
process with abort(), in every build.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
The call takes the pool's lock, so it belongs on a cold path; don't
use it inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cmempool_used_count (3),
.BR cmempool_dynamic_allocs_count (3),
.BR cmempool_allocated_bytes (3),
.BR cmempool_create_mp (3)
